<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Anconine Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1797 CE – 1798 CE · capital Ancona</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived French-backed republic in the Adriatic port of Ancona, carved from the Papal States in 1797 and merged into the Roman Republic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French troops occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-ancona">Ancona</a>, the main Adriatic port of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a>, in February 1797, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tolentino">Treaty of Tolentino</a> left them in control of it. In November 1797 local patriots, with French encouragement, proclaimed the Anconine Republic, with jurisdiction over Ancona and nearby towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was governed by a provisional administration of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobin-club">Jacobins</a> under French military control. It abolished the old papal institutions, freed the city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from the ghetto restrictions and adopted French-style laws and symbols. It also served as a base for French plans against the rest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When French troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/roman-republic-1798-1799">Roman Republic</a> (1798–1799) in February 1798, the Anconine Republic was absorbed into it in March 1798.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/anconine-republic.jpg" alt="Anconine Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: own work · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Repubblica_Anconitana.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1797 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ancona</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Repubblica Anconitana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1798&amp;focus=anconine-republic" aria-label="Anconine Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5005 631 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Anconine Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5325 887L5304 942M5325 887L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5325 887h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5325 887h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ancona. <a href="/atlas?year=1798&amp;focus=anconine-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
